{{!-- The Gate: what a reader without access reads instead of the rest of the article.

THE PLATFORM DECIDES, NOT THE POST. `post/body.hbs` chooses between the article and this card on
`{{#if access}}`, which Ghost computes per request from the reader's session against the post's
visibility and tiers. `visibility` is only what the post ASKS for; a paying member on an entitled
tier reading a `paid` post has `visibility: paid` and `access: true`, and a theme that gated on
visibility would show them a paywall they had already bought.

`visibility` is still what this card SAYS, because the pitch differs: a `members` post wants a
free account and a `paid` post wants money, and offering the wrong one is a dead end. Ghost's
`{{#has}}` reads the three cases directly, so no template arithmetic is needed.

THERE IS NO PRICE IN THE BUTTON, and the design draws one ("Upgrade — $8/month"). A theme cannot
ask the platform for a publication's entry price: `{{#get "tiers"}}` accepts `order` and `limit`
in its signature and the tiers service ignores both — `order="monthly_price ASC"` and
`order="monthly_price DESC"` return the same sequence — so "from $X" would quote whichever paid
tier the service happened to return first. A wrong price on a paywall is the worst thing this
theme could print, and Portal — which the button below opens whenever the publication has signups
to open — quotes every tier's real price against live Stripe data. Recorded as a Concession.

`data-gate` names the state for the smoke run: it is the one assertion that separates "the Gate
rendered" from "the body happened to be empty". --}}
<aside
	data-gate="{{visibility}}"
	class="border-accent-border bg-accent-soft mt-[22px] rounded-md border px-8 py-9 text-center">
	<p class="text-accent-strong font-mono text-[11px] tracking-[0.1em] uppercase">
		{{#has visibility="paid"}}{{t "Paid members only"}}{{/has}}
		{{#has visibility="members"}}{{t "Members only"}}{{/has}}
		{{#has visibility="tiers"}}{{tiers separator=", " lastSeparator=(t " and ")}}{{/has}}
	</p>

	<h2 class="font-display text-h2 tracking-heading mt-4 leading-[1.2] font-medium">
		{{#has visibility="members"}}
			{{t "Keep reading with a free account"}}
		{{else}}
			{{t "Keep reading with a paid membership"}}
		{{/has}}
	</h2>

	{{!-- The length is the platform's own reading time, computed from the whole post: Ghost gates
	the body after it counts the words, so the figure survives on a post whose body is gone.
	The second sentence is the publication's own pitch rather than the theme's — a theme that
	claims what a membership includes is guessing at someone else's product. --}}
	<p class="text-small text-muted-on-soft mx-auto mt-3.5 max-w-[46ch] leading-[1.6]">
		{{t "This post runs {length} in full." length=(reading_time seconds=(t "under a minute") minute=(t "1 minute") minutes=(t "% minutes"))}}
		{{@site.description}}
	</p>

	{{!-- A CALL TO ACTION THAT CANNOT GRANT ACCESS IS WORSE THAN NO CALL TO ACTION, which is the
	rule chrome/cta.hbs already applies to the bar's Subscribe button. Here the question is sharper,
	because what unlocks the page depends on the post as well as the publication: a `members` post
	is opened by any account, a `paid` or `tiers` post only by a paid one. Ghost's three signup
	settings answer exactly that, so each kind of post asks the one that applies to it:

		post kind      needs                    setting that says whether it is for sale
		members        any account              allow_self_signup  (signup access is "all")
		paid, tiers    a paid account           not members_invite_only  ("all" or "paid")

		signed out, `members` post, open signups   Sign up free, then Sign in beside it
		signed out, paid post, signups for sale    Choose a membership, then Sign in
		signed out, invite-only                    Sign in only — new memberships are closed
		signed out, `members` post, paid-only      Sign in only — no free account is on offer
		signed in, wrong tier                      Upgrade, into Portal's plans panel
		members off entirely                       no buttons; the card still says why it stops

	`allow_self_signup` rather than `members_enabled` for the free case: `members_enabled` is only
	`signup_access !== 'none'`, so it is true on a publication set to paid-members-only, where a
	button promising a free account promises something Portal will not create.

	`@site.signup_url` carries NO `data-portal`, and that is the point rather than an omission.
	Portal binds a click listener to every `[data-portal]` element and its handler calls
	`preventDefault()` unconditionally, so the attribute wins and the href becomes decorative — an
	anchor with `data-portal="signup"` opens Portal's signup on a publication that has none. Ghost
	computes `signup_url` from the publication's own signup access, so the state where the href must
	be obeyed is exactly the state where the attribute must be absent. The other two routes have no
	computed equivalent, so they are named and their hrefs agree with them.

	The class list repeats because these states differ in destination, attribute AND label at once,
	and grouping each state on one anchor is what makes it checkable end to end. --}}
	{{#if @site.members_enabled}}
		<div class="mt-[26px] flex flex-wrap justify-center gap-3">
			{{#if @member}}
				<a
					href="#/portal/account/plans"
					data-portal="account/plans"
					class="btn btn-primary font-ui rounded-sm px-6 py-[13px] text-[15px] font-semibold no-underline">{{t "Upgrade your membership"}}</a>
			{{else}}
				{{#has visibility="members"}}
					{{#if @site.allow_self_signup}}
						<a
							href="{{@site.signup_url}}"
							class="btn btn-primary font-ui rounded-sm px-6 py-[13px] text-[15px] font-semibold no-underline">{{t "Sign up free"}}</a>
					{{/if}}
				{{else}}
					{{#unless @site.members_invite_only}}
						<a
							href="{{@site.signup_url}}"
							class="btn btn-primary font-ui rounded-sm px-6 py-[13px] text-[15px] font-semibold no-underline">{{t "Choose a membership"}}</a>
					{{/unless}}
				{{/has}}

				<a
					href="#/portal/signin"
					data-portal="signin"
					class="btn border-text font-ui text-text hover:bg-text hover:text-surface rounded-sm border bg-transparent px-6 py-[13px] text-[15px] font-semibold no-underline">{{t "Sign in"}}</a>
			{{/if}}
		</div>
	{{/if}}

	{{!-- The writer count is the publication's, from the platform. It counts author accounts,
	which includes the owner whether or not the owner writes.

	Block params and a guard because a {{#get}} can abort: past Ghost Pro's getHelper timeout the
	helper resolves to `{authors: [], '@@ABORTED_GET_HELPER@@': true}` — no `meta` key at all — and
	still renders its block, so reading `meta.pagination.total` straight out of the context would
	print a sentence with a hole in it. The whole paragraph is inside the guard, so an empty answer
	costs no stray margin either. --}}
	{{#get "authors" limit="1" as |writers pageInfo|}}
		{{#if pageInfo.total}}
			<p class="font-ui text-muted-on-soft mt-[18px] text-[12.5px]">{{plural pageInfo.total empty="" singular=(t "Your membership supports % writer.") plural=(t "Your membership supports % writers.")}}</p>
		{{/if}}
	{{/get}}
</aside>
